        ":question/id": "b2d1a20d-340e-5980-ad18-92b97e0270ba",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-122465",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $G(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- **II.** $H(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{xy}\\,j$\n- **III.** $K(x,y,z)=x\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""
      },
