        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-122653",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y),\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)⟩$\n- **II.** $B(x,y,z)=⟨-2x,3{y}^{3},{z}^{5}⟩$\n- **III.** $C(t)=⟨2-{t}^{2},{e}^{t-1}⟩$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $A(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x+y>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$  It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""
      },
